class DecorativeGeometry:
    'This is the client-side interface to an implementation-independent\nrepresentation of "Decorations" suitable for visualization, annotation,\nlogging, or debugging but which cannot have any effect on the behavior of\na System or the evolution of a Study. DO NOT confuse this with real geometry\n(like contact geometry) which can represent physically meaningful objects that\nmay interact and change the behavior of a System. However, all geometry objects\ncan generate %DecorativeGeometry for their visualization.\n\nWhy is there a %DecorativeGeometry facility at the System level at all, so far\naway from any application program? That\'s because for crude visualization and\ndebugging purposes, the Subsystems themselves are best able to produce some\nillustrative geometry. Otherwise, you need a special purpose visualization\ntool which understands what\'s going on inside each subsystem. If you don\'t mind\ntaking what you get, just ask each subsystem to generate what it thinks would\nbe helpful visualization. To do that, the subysystems need a way to talk about\ngeometry without knowing anything about how that geometry will eventually get\nonto someone\'s screen. And that\'s why we\'re here!\n\nEach %DecorativeGeometry object has its own local coordinate system and is\ndefined self-consistently but independent of anything else. Clients can\nassociate these with a reference frame (e.g. a body), and place the local frame\nof the geometry objects on the reference frame, or at a fixed transform from\nthe reference frame. That places the %DecorativeGeometry objects in a scene. We\nsupport both 3D objects which are attached to actors in the scene, and 2D\n"screen" objects like titles which are attached to the display rather than the\nactors. The classes here deal only with the local-frame definitions of the\ngeometric objects, not their placement in the scene.\n\nThis is an abstract handle class using the PIMPL design pattern to hide the\nprivate implementation. This is effectively an abstract class although the\nvirtual function table is hidden in the private part. *'
    ...

    def getBodyId(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        "Return the *bodyId* that was supplied to the most recent setBodyId()\ncall for this %DecorativeGeometry object, or zero if that method has not been\ncalled. Copy construction and copy assignment copy the *bodyId*. This is\nintended to identify the body frame to which this geometry's placement should be\nrelative; with the default zero value meaning the Ground or World frame. *"
        ...

    def getColor(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the color specified for this object, if any, otherwise Vec3(-1)\nindicating that the default color will be used. *'
        ...

    def getFaceCamera(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Get whether the geometry acts as a billboard, always rotating to face the\ncamera. Returns 0 for no, 1 for yes, -1 for "is using default". *'
        ...

    def getIndexOnBody(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the *index* that was supplied to the most recent setIndexOnBody()\ncall for this %DecorativeGeometry object, or -1 if that method has not been\ncalled. Copy construction and copy assignment copy the *index*. Interpretation\nof this integer is up to the caller. *'
        ...

    def getLineThickness(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the line thickness specified for this object, if any, otherwise\nreturn -1 to indicate that the default line thickness should be used. *'
        ...

    def getOpacity(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the opacity specified for this object. *'
        ...

    def getRep(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any: ...

    def getRepresentation(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Returns drawing mode: -1 means "use default"; see above for others. *'
        ...

    def getResolution(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the current setting of the "resolution" factor. A return value of\n-1 means "use the default". *'
        ...

    def getScaleFactors(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the current setting of the "scale" factors. A return value of -1\nin one of the factors means to "use the default" (which is typically 1) in\nthat direction. *'
        ...

    def getTransform(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        "Return the current value of the object's transform. If none has been set\nthis will be the identity transform. Note that this transform specifies how the\npolygons are placed with respect to the object's local frame. *"
        ...

    def getUserRef(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Return the pointer value that was supplied to the most recent setUserRef()\ncall for this %DecorativeGeometry object, or zero (nullptr) if that method has\nnot been called. Copy construction and copy assignment copy the pointer.\nInterpretation of this value is up to the caller. *'
        ...

    def hasRep(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any: ...

    def implementGeometry(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any: ...

    def isEmptyHandle(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any: ...

    def isOwnerHandle(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any: ...

    def setBodyId(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        "By default the geometry should be placed relative to the Ground frame. If\nyou want it attached to another reference frame (body), say so here. The\ngeometry should be rendered with respect to the indicated body frame; however,\nthe interpretation of this integer Id is left to the implementation. If you\ndon't set the *bodyId* yourself it will be zero. For use in Simbody, the\n*bodyId* is interpreted as a MobilizedBodyIndex that can be mapped to a\nMobilizedBody that carries a reference frame.\n\nThe *bodyId* is copied if you copy the %DecorativeGeometry object. If you are\ncopying to a different body you'll need to change the bodyId afterwards. *"
        ...

    def setColor(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Request a specific color for this DecorativeGeometry object. The default\nis that the color is determined elsewhere. To explicitly request the default,\nset the color to Vec3(-1). The implementation will check the 0\'th element\n(that is, the "R" element) and if it is less than zero will ignore the other\ntwo elements and use the default for all three. *'
        ...

    def setFaceCamera(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Set whether the geometry acts as a billboard, always rotating to face the\ncamera. The default is typically no except for text. If you want 3D text that\nmoves with your model, set this to false. Here 0 means false, 1 means true,\nand -1 means "use default". *'
        ...

    def setIndexOnBody(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        "For selection or other purposes, you may want to use this method to store\nan index that can identify this particular piece of geometry. As an alternative,\nor addition, see setUserRef(). In any case the *index* is simply stored with\nthe object and returned if you ask for it. If you don't set it the value\nis -1. The *index* is copied if you copy the %DecorativeGeometry object. Be\nsure to change it afterwards if that is not the correct index for the copy.\nSimbody sets this index when %DecorativeGeometry is added to a Body. *"
        ...

    def setLineThickness(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Request an adjustment to the default rendering of lines and curves. This\ndoes NOT affect geometry generated here; it is a request passed on to the\nrenderer which will probably pass it on to the hardware. A value less\nthan or equal to zero here is interpreted as "use the default". *'
        ...

    def setOpacity(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Request a level of transparency for this DecorativeGeometry. This does NOT\naffect the generated geometry here. The default is that opacity is\ndetermined elsewhere. *'
        ...

    def setRepresentation(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Request a particular rendering representation of this DecorativeGeometry\nobject. The default is that the rendering representation choice is made\nelsewhere. *'
        ...

    def setResolution(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Each concrete %DecorativeGeometry object is expected to have a default\nresolution that gets the point across but is cheap to draw and hence probably\nsomewhat "chunky". The resolution parameter here scales that default up or\ndown. The face density in the displayed representation is roughly\nproportional to this value. 1.0 means to use the default resolution. Values\nless than 1.0 are lower resolution, and values greater than 1.0 are higher\nresolution. A value less than or equal to zero here is interpreted as an\ninstruction to "use the default". *'
        ...

    def setScale(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Convenience method to set all three scale factors to the same value. *'
        ...

    def setScaleFactors(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        'Each concrete DecorativeGeometry object is expected to have a default size\naround "1", whatever that means for a particular object, and most objects also\nallow a user-specified size on construction. The x,y,z scale factors here are\ngiven in the object\'s coordinate frame, and apply to the object as the user\nbuilt it, or to the default if the user didn\'t specify a size. The default\nscaling is 1,1,1 and any value less than or equal to zero here is interpreted\nas a request to "use the default" in that direction. *'
        ...

    def setTransform(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        "This transform shifts the generated polygons with respect to this object's\nlocal frame. Subsequent calls with other transforms simply replace the earlier\none; they do not accumulate. The default transform is identity and you can call\nsetTransform(Transform()) to put the transform back into its original state.\nThis value affects the generated polygonal data. *"
        ...

    def setUserRef(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any:
        "Use this method to store an arbitrary reference pointer with this\n%DecorativeGeometry object. This is particularly useful in selection operations\nwhere the rendering of this object has been picked by a user and you want to\nmap it back to some meaningful object in your model. You can also use the\nsetIndexOnBody() method to store some additional identifying information.\nIf you don't set this pointer it will be set to zero (nullptr). This value\nis stored and returned only; no interpretation is done and the pointed-to\nobject will not be deleted when the %DecorativeGeometry object is deleted.\n\nWarning: The value of the *userRef* pointer is copied if you make a copy of the\n%DecorativeGeometry object. That is likely to be incorrect in many\ncircumstances, depending on how you are using this value. Be sure to clear or\nchange the pointer if necessary after you make a copy. *"
        ...

    def updRep(self, *args: Any, **kwargs: Any) -> Any: ...

